WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.220
Each summer, the Chesapeake Bay's deep channel typically has too little oxygen near

2
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.940
the bottom. Crabs and fish cannot stay.

3
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:12.940
People call it a dead zone.

4
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.480
No one pours in a chemical that soaks up oxygen.

5
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.740
So how can fertilizer on fields far up rivers such as the Susquehanna, Potomac, and

6
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.860
James take oxygen from the Bay's floor?

7
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:26.820
Can people fix it?

8
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:27.683
No one pours in a chemical that soaks up oxygen.

9
00:00:27.683 --> 00:00:31.603
A watershed is all the land that drains into one river, lake or bay.

10
00:00:32.563 --> 00:00:36.663
Runoff is rainwater that flows over the ground and carries what it picks up.

11
00:00:37.443 --> 00:00:42.623
Part of what feeds the dead zone washes in from farms, lawns, wastewater plants and

12
00:00:42.623 --> 00:00:47.123
even the air. A nutrient is a substance living things need to grow.

13
00:00:48.003 --> 00:00:49.963
Here, that means nitrogen and phosphorus.

14
00:00:51.067 --> 00:00:53.467
Extra nutrients make algae grow fast.

15
00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:56.887
A sudden, thick growth of algae is an algal bloom.

16
00:00:57.747 --> 00:01:02.447
The green water blocks sunlight from underwater grasses, and algae growing on the

17
00:01:02.447 --> 00:01:03.887
grass blades shade them too.

18
00:01:04.787 --> 00:01:06.687
Then the algae die and sink to the bottom.

19
00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:10.847
A decomposer is a living thing that breaks down dead matter.

20
00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:16.090
Bacteria decompose the dead algae, and they use oxygen as they do.

21
00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.130
Dissolved oxygen is oxygen gas mixed into water.

22
00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:22.990
Fish and crabs take it in through their gills.

23
00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.790
Low oxygen in bottom water is called hypoxia.

24
00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:29.250
Animals that can swim away leave.

25
00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:31.350
Others may sicken or die.

26
00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.510
The fertilizer never takes oxygen itself.

27
00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:36.710
Living things use it.

28
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.240
Why the deep channel, and why summer?

29
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.940
In summer, fresh river water floats on saltier, heavier seawater.

30
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.220
The layers mix poorly, so little new oxygen reaches the bottom.

31
00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:54.420
Warm water also holds less dissolved oxygen, and bacteria decompose faster when it

32
00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.980
is warm, so they use oxygen faster.

33
00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:01.100
Eutrophication is the enrichment of water with nutrients.

34
00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.220
It feeds too much algae growth and can lead to oxygen loss.

35
00:02:06.283 --> 00:02:09.803
So how do human actions change the Bay, and what can we do?

36
00:02:10.623 --> 00:02:13.843
People add many of those nutrients, so people can cut them.

37
00:02:14.703 --> 00:02:18.683
A riparian forest buffer is a strip of trees and shrubs along a stream.

38
00:02:19.543 --> 00:02:23.303
It slows runoff and traps soil, and its roots hold the bank in place.

39
00:02:24.463 --> 00:02:28.783
Plants and soil microbes also remove nitrate from water seeping through the ground

40
00:02:28.783 --> 00:02:33.363
beneath it. Cover crops and wastewater upgrades also cut nutrients.

41
00:02:33.363 --> 00:02:38.723
Oyster reefs shelter other animals, but they do not replace cutting nutrients.

42
00:02:39.743 --> 00:02:41.563
Underwater grass has partly returned.

43
00:02:42.543 --> 00:02:47.563
Recovery is real, but slow and usually partial, and results often lag by years.
